4. None other defendants entered appearance.&nbsp; 

 4. Is the suit bad for mis-joinder and non-joinder of parties ?&nbsp; 

 8. The learned Advocate Ms Karishma Jahan appearing on behalf of the plaintiff at the preliminary while taken this Court through the depositions of the PWs and DW argued that only question to be decided in this suit is whether there has been late delivery of the cargo by the carrier and whether the plaintiff has suffered any loss due to such non-delivery of the cargo in time and as such the issues framed earlier in this suit is required to be re-cast to that extent. In elaborating this argument she stated that the exhibited Bills of Lading, as issued by the Master of the defendant No. 1 vessel, is a clear proof that the plaintiff has boarded its cargo on the defendant No. 1 vessel `MT Komandarm Fedko&#39; at the loading port on the charter party stipulated date 20-7-1999 and admittedly the defendant No. 1 vessel carried the said cargo up to the Chittagong Port. Therefore, the defendant No. 1 vessel has to reach by the date at the discharge port as stipulated in the Bills of Lading. Moreover the normal period required in traveling the distance in between the load port San Lorenge, Argentina to discharge port Chittagong is 40 days but since the vessel admittedly reached Chittagong Port after a total period of 121 days, the defendant is liable to compensate the loss &amp; damage suffered by the plaintiff which is apparent on the facts and circumstances that plaintiff edible oil refining mill was idle for the non-availability of the raw material as the plaintiff had to pay the staff salary, the gas and electricity bills and to incur other expenses.&nbsp; 

 28. The exhibited document evidenced that the vessel &#39;MT Komandram Fedko&#39; was built in the year 1976 and although none of the parties have adduced any evidence as to the distance of San Lorenzo, Argentina to Chittagong a judicial notice on the Google map, as visited through our laptop available to me right now here, has indicated that the distance is about 9300 nautical miles and a sea-going vessel having the dead weight like the vessel &#39;MT Komandarm Fedko&#39; shall have a speed of 9 to 15 nautical miles per hour which shows that such a vessel will take 45 to 50 days in covering the distance of 9300 nautical mile, if it proceeds with its minimum speed i.e. in 9 nautical miles per hour. Taking the age of the vessel `MT Komandarm Fedko&#39; as a old aged vessel and the peril of the sea and other expected compulsion, it appears that the defendant-vessel should cover the distance of 9300 nautical mile maximum within 50 days. But as the Exhibit No. Z (14), the, tanker voyage charter party agreement, evidenced that there was other cargo in the said vessel to be delivered at Chennai, India as such the days to be included in reaching Chittagong will also include the days that has been expended at Mumbai although such a deviation was in stipulation in between the plaintiff and the supplier, the defendant No. 6. But the defendant No. 2 failed to adduce any evidence as to the days required to deliver the cargo at Chennai and, therefore, reasonably taking the said days as 10 days maximum, this Court finds that to reach Chittagong Port it cannot exceed 60 days. Therefore, this Court finds that as the defendant No. 2 has taken 121 days to reach Chittagong Port, it has come late to Chittagong Port by 61 days.&nbsp; 

 39. The PW 1 proved the averments made in the plaint that the defendant No. 6 assured the plaintiff though its different correspondence, being exhibit No. X (series) regarding the ETA of the vessel at Chittagong. None of the correspondence was made by the defendant No. 3. Therefore, it has been amply proved that the defendant No. 6 made these correspondence with the plaintiff not only as the supplier of the cargo but also as the charter of the vessel -`MT Komandarm Fedko&#39;. But it appears from the exhibit E, F, G, H, I and J, the Bills of lading, that the same have been issued by captain P Chkhartisivili as per Master&#39;s authority. Such singing of the Bills of lading by the Master (or under Master&#39;s authority) shall be treated as singing under his usual capacity as agent of the ship owner. This charter party agreement Exhibit- Z(14), since voyage charter party agreement and entered into by the shipper with the shipowner for carriage of his cargo, the ship owner remained the legal carrier of the consignment as argued by the great author CARVER in his treatise &#39;Carriage by Sea&#39; that in &quot;such charter party agreement the ship-owner remained the operator of the ship by providing the service of Master and the crew members as his own employee retaining the possession of the ship with him and the Master acts as his agent being the servant of the shipownec. One of the implication of voyage charter party agreement renders that the shipowner undertakes that the ship shall proceed on her voyage to the port of discharge with reasonable dispatch and the same must be prosecuted without unnecessary delay or deviation. The implied undertaking of the shipowner that he will be diligent in carrying the goods on the agreed voyage and will do so directly without any unnecessary deviation is to be looked into with reference to the other stipulation that involves with the charter party agreement.&nbsp;
